    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i was looking at my student loan statement and the principal isnt going down as much as i thought it would even though i pay the same amount every month. can u explain how the interest actually works with the balance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you make a fixed monthly payment on a student loan, the money is split into two parts: the interest that has accrued since your last payment, and the remaining amount that goes toward the principal. Early in the loan, the interest portion is large because the balance is high, so only a small sliver of your payment actually reduces the principal. As the balance shrinks, the interest portion gets smaller, and more of your payment goes to principal—this is called amortization. If your payment is exactly the minimum, the principal will barely move at first, which is why it feels like you're treading water.",
      "source": "target",
