    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense but it still feels like there are these hidden things eating my money. like i pay $300 but the balance only drops by like $20. is there any way to calculate exactly how much of each payment is being \"lost\" to interest versus hitting the actual loan? i want to see the math behind it.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You’re absolutely right to want the math—it’s not hidden, but it’s buried in the fine print. The formula is: **Interest for the month = (Annual Interest Rate ÷ 12) × Current Principal Balance**. So if your loan has a 6% annual rate, your monthly rate is 0.005 (6% ÷ 12). Multiply that by your current balance—say $20,000—and you get $100 in interest for that month. If you pay $300, then $100 goes to interest and $200 goes to principal. The reason your principal only dropped by $20 is that your balance is much higher than $20,000, or your rate is higher, or both. You can check your statement for the exact \"interest rate\" and \"current balance,\" then plug them into that formula to see the split for any given month. To see the full amortization schedule, use an online calculator—it will show you every payment’s interest/principal breakdown over the life of the loan.",
      "source": "target",
